 "speeches": [
  {
   "slug": "speech-358385",
   "speaker": "Paul Fletcher",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2018-02-07",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The Road Vehicle Standards Bill 2018 and its transitional bill were moved for second reading, aiming to modernize vehicle import regulation with a 12‑month transition period and repeal the Motor Vehicle Standards Act 1989."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-363704",
   "speaker": "Anthony Albanese",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2018-05-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "Labor supported the Road Vehicle Standards package, which replaces the Motor Vehicle Standards Act, creates an online register of approved vehicles, and is projected to reduce industry compliance costs by $68 million, while noting stakeholder concerns about impacts on small workshops and campervan converters."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-363705",
   "speaker": "Craig Kelly",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2018-05-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The Road Vehicle Standards Bill 2018 was commended, with the argument that Labor must detail its 45% transport emissions reduction plan and that road safety should be prioritised over emission targets, citing 1,300 annual road deaths and $27 billion in road trauma costs."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-363706",
   "speaker": "Stephen Jones",
   "party": "Labor",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2018-05-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The Road Vehicle Standards Bill 2018 and related bills are supported as a bipartisan update to the Motor Vehicle Standards Act 1989, replacing physical compliance plates with online registers and strengthening enforcement, with estimated compliance costs of $68 million a year. The speaker also announced Labor's policy to require car manufacturers to share technical information with independent mechanics on commercially fair terms."
  },
  {
   "slug": "speech-363707",
   "speaker": "Paul Fletcher",
   "party": "Liberal",
   "state": "federal",
   "date": "2018-05-30",
   "stage_hint": null,
   "brief": "The Road Vehicle Standards Bill 2018 and four companion bills were presented as a modern framework for regulating road vehicles, projected to save industry an estimated $20 million, establish the Register of Approved Vehicles (saving around $14 million a year), and introduce compulsory recall powers. The government announced amendments to allow used-vehicle imports for campervan/motorhome conversions, adjust power-to-weight thresholds for specialist vehicles, permit left-hand-drive heavy vehicles, and presented addenda to the explanatory memoranda following the Scrutiny of Bills committee's recommendations."
  }
 ],
